The farmer has only one animal, the goat, and a boat that can carry the farmer and one animal. To get the goat across the river, the farmer simply takes the goat in the boat and crosses the river once. This single trip moves both the farmer and the goat to the other side, fulfilling the requirement of getting the goat across. No additional trips are necessary since there are no other animals or constraints mentioned that would require the farmer to return or make multiple crossings.

Thus, it takes **1 trip** to get the goat across the river.

\boxed{1}

The farmer, goat, and boat are all on the starting side of the river. The boat can carry the farmer and one animal, which in this case is the goat. To get the goat across the river, the farmer simply gets into the boat with the goat and rows to the other side. This requires only one trip, as the goat is transported directly to the destination side in a single crossing.

There are no other animals or items mentioned, so no constraints or additional trips are needed. The minimum number of trips to get the goat across is one.

\boxed{1}
